  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Doha technical session on July 1–2 concluded without a scheduled date for the High Level Committee established under the Lake Lucerne communiqué of June 22. The Qatar-Pakistan joint communiqué released July 2 stated the next meeting 'will take place after the conclusion of funeral processions for former Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei.' The state funeral procession runs July 4 (Tehran), July 5 (Qom), July 6–8 (Karbala route), and July 9 (final interment at Mashhad). Iran's Supreme National Security Council issued a protocol directive suspending official diplomatic engagements through July 9. The Doha session addressed logistics and financial incentives only; the nuclear agenda was deferred in full. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span>, <span class="cite">F13</span></p>

      <p>Majlis Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf appeared on Iranian state television July 2 and stated: 'Talk of IAEA inspectors' access to bombed sites is false. No access will be granted to Fordow, Natanz, or Isfahan.' The position was codified in a Majlis joint committee resolution passed June 23 and a Supreme National Security Council decree. This created a documented institutional contradiction with US negotiator Vance's Bürgenstock claim of June 21 that Iran had agreed to IAEA inspections — a gap left unaddressed at Doha. With F8's July 5 horizon at 2 days and Iran's refusal anchored in law, F8 was resolved NO. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>Iran's joint military command issued a public statement July 2, carried by CNN and CNBC, warning that commercial vessels transiting via the Oman/IMO-designated coastal route in the Strait of Hormuz without prior IRGC authorization face 'immediate and firm response.' The statement came six days after the June 26 IRGC drone engagement against a Singapore-flagged cargo ship — the first confirmed IRGC attack on a commercial vessel since the June 16 MCM commencement and a documented ceasefire violation under the Islamabad MOU framework. F12 was opened as a new 14-day forecast tracking IRGC enforcement action on the Oman/IMO route; F12 YES is the primary downside scenario for F11. → moved <span class="cite">F12</span></p>

      <p>Single-day commercial transits through the Strait of Hormuz reached approximately 27 vessels on July 2, per MarineTraffic AIS tracking data — the highest single-day count since the conflict began in late February. The 7-day moving average through July 2 was estimated at 15–20 vessels per day, below F11's 30/day resolution threshold. F11 was adjusted upward from 0.38 to 0.42, accepting the DA's 60-day runway and single-day trajectory arguments, weighted against the new IRGC route warning, war-risk insurance at 8× pre-crisis (six P&amp;I clubs maintaining exclusions), and the contested 40-day versus 6-month mine clearance timelines. → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset legal adviser issued a formal recommendation on July 3 that July 17 is the earliest legally feasible date for a second reading of the dissolution bill — two days after F6's July 15 horizon deadline. Parliamentary summer recess is scheduled to begin approximately July 16. Times of Israel (July 3) reported the election is expected to proceed on the original October 27 date. No second or third reading of the dissolution bill was scheduled as of July 3; the haredi-coalition dispute over a September versus October election date was not resolved, and no plenum session calendar was published. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
