  </div>

  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and IRIB coverage through June 10 – July 3 consistently framed the February 28 death of Ali Khamenei as martyrdom (shahid) under US military aggression. State funeral coverage on July 3 used martyrdom language uniformly across IRNA, IRIB, Fars, and Tasnim. Mojtabi Khamenei, confirmed as Supreme Leader by the Assembly of Experts on March 9, has appeared in state media only in pre-recorded addresses; he has not addressed the Islamabad MOU framework, the IAEA dispute, or the HLC process in any public broadcast as of July 3. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Tasnim and Fars framed the June 17 Islamabad MOU as an Iranian diplomatic achievement and a cessation of US bombardment — with no reference to nuclear concessions or IAEA access commitments. Ghalibaf's July 2 IAEA statement was headlined 'Iran: No inspector access to martyred sites' across IRNA and IRIB, embedding the nuclear-access dispute in the martyrdom frame. IRNA's coverage of the Doha July 1–2 session described Iranian technical teams 'reviewing US compliance with MOU commitments,' framing Iran as the evaluator rather than the negotiated party; Pakistan was not named in any IRNA dispatch from Doha. The martyrdom-and-evaluator framing narrows the available space for any face-saving IAEA access formula before August 17. → supports <span class="cite">F10</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

